<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scholar-officials</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Scholar-officials were the educated class of imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> who staffed the civil service after passing the state examinations based on the Confucian classics. They combined literary learning with administrative responsibility and enjoyed high social prestige. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> describe them as the backbone of Ming governance and society.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scholar-officials.jpg" alt="Scholar-officials" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Jiang_Shunfu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Large landowners evaded taxation by absorbing peasant smallholders into dependent tenancy, eroding the tax base while concentrating private military capacity outside state control, while bitter feuds between court eunuchs and <b>scholar-officials</b> paralysed the court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Society was organised hierarchically, with the emperor at the apex, followed by <b>scholar-officials</b> recruited through the civil service examinations, the local gentry, peasants and artisans, while merchants held an ambiguous status despite their wealth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>scholar-officials</b>, the literati, were the governing class of China, and their education in the classics, history, poetry and calligraphy defined elite culture.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty’s own Banner and Green Standard armies had failed against them, and the war was being won by new regional armies raised by Han Chinese <b>scholar-officials</b>: the Xiang Army of Zeng Guofan and the Huai Army of Li Hongzhang.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Aristocracy also differs from monarchy, the rule of one, and from a bureaucracy recruited by merit such as that of imperial China, whose <b>scholar-officials</b> owed their posts to examinations rather than to birth.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>A king who defied the <b>scholar-officials</b> could be overthrown: Yeonsangun, who abolished the Office of Censors and purged scholars, was deposed in 1506, and Gwanghaegun in 1623.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li></ul></section>
